The political and economic crises of the late 1850s and early 1860s led to a resurgence of the workers' movement across Europe.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|p=35}} In Britain, a new generation of trade union leaders, known as the "[[New Model Union|Junta]]," emerged, advocating for greater political engagement and solidarity.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|p=36}} They organized solidarity actions for various international causes, including support for [[Giuseppe Garibaldi]]'s struggle for Italian independence, opposition to [[January Uprising|Russian repression in Poland]], and support for the Union side in the [[American Civil War]].{{sfn|Braunthal|1966|pp=85–87}} In France, where trade unions were illegal but tolerated as [[friendly societies]], workers influenced by [[Mutualism (economic theory)|Proudhonism]] began to seek independent political representation, culminating in the "Manifesto of the Sixty" in 1864, which was written by [[Henri Tolain]] and proclaimed the existence of a distinct proletariat class.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1p=41|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=105}} Initial contacts between British and French workers' leaders, established during the [[1862 International Exhibition|1862 London International Exhibition]], led to a meeting in London in July 1863 that set the stage for a new international organization.{{sfnm|1a1=Katz|1y=1992|1p=2|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2pp=87–89}} The preparatory ''Address of English to French Workmen'', drafted by trade union leader [[George Odger]], articulated the need for international cooperation to prevent the importation of foreign workers to break strikes:
{{quote|A fraternity of peoples is highly necessary for the cause of labour, for we find that whenever we attempt to better our social condition by reducing the hours of toil, or by raising the price of labour, our employers threaten us with bringing over Frenchmen, Germans, Belgians and others to do our work at a reduced rate of wages; and we are sorry to say that this has been done, though not from any desire on the part of our continental brethren to injure us, but through a want of regular and systematic communication between the industrial classes of all countries. Our aim is to bring up the wages of the ill-paid to as near a level as possible with that of those who are better remunerated, and not to allow our employers to play us off one against the other, and so drag us down to the lowest possible condition, suitable to their avaricious bargaining.{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1pp=1–2|2a1=Katz|2y=1992|2p=4}}}}

[[File:International-founding-1864.jpg|thumb|Depiction of the IWA's founding meeting at [[St Martin's Hall]] in London, 1864]]
On 28 September 1864, a great international meeting was held in [[St Martin's Hall]] in London, attended by some 2,000 workers. The event was primarily a joint affair of British and French labor leaders, including Odger and Tolain.{{sfnm|1a1=Katz|1y=1992|1p=2|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=105}} The assembly unanimously resolved to found an international organization of workers, with its central committee, which became known as the General Council, to be based in London.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1p=46|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=93}} A committee of twenty-one was elected to draft the rules and program. The committee included prominent British trade union leaders like Odger, [[William Randal Cremer]], and [[George Howell (trade unionist)|George Howell]]; French representatives Tolain, [[Charles Limousin]], [[Eugène Varlin]], and [[Eugène Dupont]]; and exiled radicals including the Italian [[Giuseppe Mazzini|Mazzinist]] [[Luigi Wolff]] and the German socialist [[Karl Marx]].{{sfnm|1a1=Katz|1y=1992|1pp=2–3, 6, 8–9|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=107}} The meeting was presided over by the English [[Positivist]] professor [[Edward Spencer Beesly]].{{sfn|Katz|1992|p=5}} Though he was not an organizer of the meeting and sat "in a non-speaking capacity on the platform", Marx was invited to join the committee by the German tailor [[Johann Eccarius]], who was an active figure in the British trade union movement, and played a decisive role from the outset.{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=5|2a1=Stekloff|2y=1928|2p=46|3a1=Katz|3y=1992|3p=6|4a1=Cole|4y=1954|4p=107|5a1=Braunthal|5y=1966|5p=92}}

[[File:Marx1867 (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|[[Karl Marx]]]]
Marx drafted the two foundational documents of the association: the ''Inaugural Address of the International Working Men's Association'' and the ''Provisional Rules of the Association''.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|p=47}} After early drafts by Mazzinists and [[Owenism|Owenites]] were rejected, Marx's versions were enthusiastically accepted.{{sfnm|1a1=Katz|1y=1992|1p=14|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=101}} The ''Address'' analyzed the condition of the working class since 1848, highlighting the stark contrast between the growth of capitalist wealth and the persistence of working-class misery. Marx was careful to avoid language that would alienate the organization's diverse founders.{{sfnm|1a1=Cole|1y=1954|1p=108|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=99}} He pointed to two victories for the "[[political economy]] of the working class": the legislative success of the ten-hours day in Britain and the growth of the co-operative movement.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1p=47|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=108|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=102}} It concluded that to achieve emancipation, the workers must conquer political power and that their success depended on international solidarity, proclaiming the famous slogan from ''The Communist Manifesto'': "[[Proletarians of all countries, unite!]]".{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1pp=77, 79|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=103}} The ''Preamble to the Provisional Rules'' set forth the core principles: that the emancipation of the working classes must be achieved by the workers themselves, and that this economic emancipation was the "great end to which every political movement ought to be subordinate as a means."{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=265|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=104}}

=== Internal conflicts and ideological development ===
The IWA was an ideologically heterogeneous body, encompassing a wide range of [[Socialism|socialist]], [[Communism|communist]], and [[Anarchism|anarchist]] viewpoints.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|p=75}} Its history was marked by a continuous struggle between its various factions, primarily between the communists (or [[Marxism|Marxists]]) and the anti-statist wings, first the [[Mutualism (economic theory)|mutualists]] (followers of [[Pierre-Joseph Proudhon]]) and later the [[Collectivist anarchism|collectivist]]s (followers of [[Mikhail Bakunin]]).{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|pp=75–76}}
==== Proudhonists versus communists ====
[[File:Portrait Pierre-Joseph Proudhon (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|[[Pierre-Joseph Proudhon]]]]
The French members of the International were initially dominated by followers of [[Pierre-Joseph Proudhon]].{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1p=67|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=98}} Proudhonism, or [[Mutualism (economic theory)|mutualism]], was a form of [[market socialism]] based on the idea of "free credit" and "equitable exchange" among small independent producers.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1p=67|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=111|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=98}} The Proudhonists were staunchly opposed to political action, strikes, trade unions, and the collective ownership of property. They viewed the state as an oppressive institution to be ignored rather than captured, and they opposed any form of centralized organization.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1pp=68, 81|2a1=Katz|2y=1992|2p=8|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=124}} This put them in direct conflict with the General Council, particularly with Marx, who advocated for a program of communism, political action through an independent workers' party, and the necessity of the strike as a weapon in the class struggle.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|pp=76–77}}

The first congresses of the International became battlegrounds for these opposing views. At the London Conference of 1865, the Parisian mutualists put forward a program based on Proudhon's ideas, but it was largely overshadowed by the General Council's Marx-penned agenda.{{sfnm|1a1=Katz|1y=1992|1p=16|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=121}} At the [[Geneva Congress (1866)|Geneva Congress]] (1866), the Proudhonists were largely defeated. The congress passed resolutions, based on Marx's instructions to the General Council delegates, affirming the importance of trade unions, advocating for the eight-hour day and the abolition of child labour, and recognizing the need for legislative intervention in the economy.{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1pp=32, 83–87|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2pp=125–126|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3pp=124–126}} At the [[Lausanne Congress (1867)|Lausanne Congress]] (1867), the question of property was debated, with the first resolutions favouring state ownership of transport and exchange being passed.{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1pp=38, 169|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=128|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=129}} The final victory for the communists came at the [[Brussels Congress (1868)|Brussels Congress]] (1868), which passed a resolution declaring that mines, quarries, railways, and arable land should become the common property of society, to be operated by producers' co-operatives and federated communes.{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=40|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=143|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=134}} The resolution stated:
{{quote|That the quarries, collieries, and other mines, as well as the railways, ought in a normal state of society to belong to the community represented by the state, a state itself subject to the laws of justice. [...] The Congress thinks that the economical development of modern society will create the social necessity of converting arable land into the common property of society, and of letting the soil on behalf of the state to agricultural companies under conditions analogous to those stated in regard to mines and railways.{{sfn|Musto|2014|p=91}}}}
At the time, this turn towards communism was not widely associated with Marx, whose economic theories were still relatively unknown among the delegates.{{sfn|Katz|1992|p=40}}

==== Bakunin and the anarchists ====
[[File:Mikhail Bakunin Nader (3x4 cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|[[Mikhail Bakunin]]]]
In 1868, the anti-statist opposition to the General Council coalesced around the Russian revolutionary [[Mikhail Bakunin]], who joined the Geneva section of the International.{{sfn|Musto|2014|p=42}} Bakunin and his followers, known as [[Collectivist anarchism|collectivist]]s or anarchists, while agreeing with the Marxists on the need for collective ownership of property, radically diverged on the question of the state and political action.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1pp=160–161|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=139}} Bakunin advocated for the complete abolition of the state and all forms of political authority, which he saw as inherently oppressive. He rejected the Marxist concept of the "[[dictatorship of the proletariat]]," arguing that any state, even a "[[Socialist state|workers' state]]," would inevitably become a new form of tyranny.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1pp=176, 179|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=134|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=176}} The primary means for social change, in Bakunin's view, was not organized political action but the spontaneous revolutionary instincts of the masses, particularly the peasantry and the ''[[Lumpenproletariat]]''.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1pp=161, 164|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=175}}

Bakunin established a secret society within the IWA called the "[[International Alliance of Socialist Democracy]]".{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=42|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=178}} When the Alliance applied to join the International as an organized body with its own program and leadership, the General Council refused in December 1868, describing it as "a second international body operating within and outside the International Working Men’s Association" and a means for "the intrigues of every race and nationality".{{sfn|Katz|1992|pp=54–55}} Although the Alliance officially disbanded in March 1869, its sections were admitted as regular branches of the International, and the secret network continued to operate, aiming to win over the International to Bakunin's program.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1pp=157, 173–174|2a1=Katz|2y=1992|2p=55|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3pp=178–179}}

[[File:Baselerkongress 1869.jpg|thumb|Depiction of delegates at the [[Basel Congress (1869)|1869 Basel Congress]]]]
The conflict came to a head at the [[Basel Congress (1869)|Basel Congress]] (1869). While the congress reaffirmed the Brussels resolution on the collectivization of land, a major clash occurred over the right of [[inheritance]].{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1p=141|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=146|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=136}} Bakunin argued for its immediate and complete abolition as a prerequisite for social change.{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=161|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=147|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=138}} Marx, through the General Council's report, argued that the abolition of inheritance would be the ''result'' of the abolition of private property, not its cause, and proposed transitional measures like increased inheritance taxes.{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=164|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=147|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=138}} Bakunin's proposal received 32 votes for, 23 against, and 13 abstentions, failing to achieve the necessary majority. The General Council's proposal was defeated with 19 votes for, 37 against, and 6 abstentions.{{sfnm|1a1=Katz|1y=1992|1p=62|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=148|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=138}} The outcome was seen as a victory for Bakunin and a demonstration of his growing influence, though his motion had also failed.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|p=144}} In a surprising move, however, Bakunin voted with the Marxists to grant the General Council greater powers, including the right to suspend sections that acted against the International's interests, perhaps because the Council had recently accepted his Alliance's Geneva section into membership.{{sfnm|1a1=Cole|1y=1954|1p=148|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2pp=177, 203}}

=== Franco-Prussian War and the Paris Commune ===
The period following the Basel Congress represented the zenith of the International's influence and membership.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|p=147}} The IWA was deeply involved in the burgeoning strike movement across the continent, offering financial and organizational support and preventing the importation of strikebreakers, which greatly enhanced its prestige among the working class.{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=34|2a1=Katz|2y=1992|2p=26|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3pp=114–115}}

The outbreak of the [[Franco-Prussian War]] in July 1870 was a severe blow to the International.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|p=184}} The General Council, in a manifesto written by Marx, denounced the war as a dynastic conflict and called upon French and German workers to oppose their governments.{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1pp=49, 236–239|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=143}} After the defeat of Napoleon III and the proclamation of the [[French Third Republic|French Republic]], the General Council issued a second manifesto, warning the German workers against a war of conquest and protesting the planned annexation of [[Alsace–Lorraine|Alsace-Lorraine]].{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=240|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=146}} While German social democratic leaders [[August Bebel]] and [[Wilhelm Liebknecht]] abstained from the initial war credits vote, their principled anti-war stance earned them arrest and prosecution.{{sfnm|1a1=Katz|1y=1992|1pp=70–71|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=159|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=148}}

[[File:Barricade18March1871.jpg|thumb|Barricade during the [[Paris Commune]] of 1871]]
The most significant event of this period was the [[Paris Commune]] of 1871. Although the International as an organization did not instigate the Commune, its Parisian members played an influential role.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1p=194|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=153}} Around a third of the members of the Commune's council were members of the International, including prominent figures like [[Eugène Varlin]], [[Benoît Malon]], and [[Leó Frankel]]. They formed part of the minority faction, advocating for social and economic reforms and opposing the [[Jacobin (politics)|Jacobin]]-[[Blanquism|Blanquist]] majority's focus on purely political and military measures.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1pp=194-195|2a1=Katz|2y=1992|2p=76|3a1=Cole|3y=1954|3p=160|4a1=Braunthal|4y=1966|4p=153}} Following the bloody suppression of the Commune, Marx, on behalf of the General Council, wrote ''[[The Civil War in France]]'', a powerful defense of the Commune that hailed it as the first historical example of the "dictatorship of the proletariat" and a political form for the economic emancipation of labour.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1pp=196–197|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=180|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3pp=154–155}} The fall of the Commune dealt a heavy blow to the IWA, ushering in a period of harsh reaction and government persecution across Europe, including a legal ban on the International in France.{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=52|2a1=Katz|2y=1992|2p=112|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3pp=156–157, 182}} The British trade union leaders, who had never been revolutionaries, recoiled from Marx's defense of the Commune and distanced themselves from the organization.{{sfnm|1a1=Cole|1y=1954|1p=179|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=173}}

=== Split at the Hague Congress ===
The aftermath of the Paris Commune intensified the internal conflict within the International. The experience of the Commune reinforced the Marxist view on the necessity of an independent political party of the working class to conquer state power.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|p=203}} This was formally expressed at the London Conference of 1871, which passed a resolution declaring that "against this collective power of the propertied classes the working class cannot act, as a class, except by constituting itself into a political party, distinct from, and opposed to, all old parties formed by the propertied classes".{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=56|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=215|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=180}}

This resolution was a direct challenge to the anarchists. The Bakuninist [[Jura Federation]], at its Sonvillier Congress in November 1871, issued [[Sonvilier Circular|a circular]] to all federations denouncing the London Conference's decision as an imposition of "authoritarian" German communist doctrine and calling for the replacement of the centralized General Council with a "free federation of autonomous groups."{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=39|2a1=Katz|2y=1992|2p=97|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=183}} The [[Spanish Regional Federation of the IWA|Spanish]], Italian, and Belgian federations, where Bakuninist influence was strong, rallied to the side of the Jurassians.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|pp=213-217}}

[[File:Marx and Engels at Hague Congress.jpg|thumb|Depiction of Marx and Engels at the [[Hague Congress (1872)|Hague Congress]], 1872]]
The final showdown occurred at the [[Hague Congress (1872)|Hague Congress]] in September 1872.{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=61|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=184}} The congress was the most representative in the International's history, with 65 delegates from across Europe, America, and even Australia.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|p=244}} The Marxist faction secured a majority, albeit a contested one, thanks to a large delegation from London holding numerous mandates from sympathetic groups.{{sfnm|1a1=Katz|1y=1992|1p=129|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=216}} The congress reaffirmed the London Conference's resolution on political action, incorporating it into the Association's rules as Article 7a.{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1pp=62, 268|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=187}} Following the report of a special committee, Bakunin and [[James Guillaume]] were expelled from the International for their involvement in a secret society (the Alliance) and for fraudulent activities, including an attempt at intimidation related to Bakunin's unfinished translation of ''[[Das Kapital]]''.{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=66|2a1=Katz|2y=1992|2p=135|3a1=Cole|3y=1954|3pp=211–213|4a1=Braunthal|4y=1966|4p=187}} In a final, decisive move proposed by Engels, the congress voted to transfer the seat of the General Council from London to New York City.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1p=234|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=218|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=187}} This decision, intended to save the Council from falling into the hands of either the Blanquists or the anarchists, effectively marked the end of the First International as a functioning European organization.{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1pp=67, 68|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=219|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3pp=189–190}}

=== After the split: two Internationals ===

The transfer of the General Council to New York proved fatal to the Marxist wing of the International. Cut off from the main currents of the European workers' movement, the new Council, led by [[Friedrich Sorge]], found its influence rapidly waning.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|p=268}} In Europe, only Germany showed continued adherence, but the nascent [[Social Democratic Party of Germany|Social Democratic Party]] was focused on its national development.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|p=274}} In Britain, the movement split and collapsed, with its former leaders drifting into liberal reformism.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|pp=271–272}} The Geneva Congress of 1873, the only one held under the New York Council's authority, was a "pitiful affair" composed almost entirely of Swiss delegates and described by Marx as an "inevitable 'fiasco'".{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=59|2a1=Katz|2y=1992|2p=143|3a1=Cole|3y=1954|3p=220|4a1=Braunthal|4y=1966|4p=191}} Recognizing the futility of continuing the organization in its old form, Marx and Engels supported its formal dissolution.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|p=281}} The final act took place at a conference in Philadelphia on 15 July 1876, where the International Workingmen's Association was officially disbanded, with the delegates proclaiming that "the International is dead!" but also expressing confidence that "the principles of the organisation [are] recognised and defended by the progressive working men of the entire civilised world."{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=301|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=191}}

Immediately following the Hague Congress, the expelled anarchist wing held [[St. Imier Congress|its own congress at St. Imier, Switzerland]].{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=59|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=190}} They rejected the Hague resolutions, declared the General Council abolished, and established [[Anti-authoritarian International|a new International]] based on a "pact of friendship, solidarity and mutual defence" and the principles of federalism and the "autonomy of sections."{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1p=294|2a1=Katz|2y=1992|2p=137}} The St. Imier Congress resolution on the political action of the proletariat stated:

{{quote|1. That the destruction of all political power is the first task of the proletariat;<br/>2. That any organization of so-called provisional and revolutionary political power to bring about such destruction can only be a further deception, and would be as dangerous to the proletariat as all governments existing today;<br/>3. That, rejecting all compromise to reach the fulfilment of social Revolution, the proletarians of all countries must establish, outside of all bourgeois politics, the solidarity of revolutionary action.{{sfn|Musto|2014|p=294}}}}

This "anti-authoritarian" International, supported by the [[Spanish Regional Federation of the IWA|Spanish]], Italian, Belgian, and Jura federations, held several congresses (Geneva 1873, Brussels 1874, Berne 1876).{{sfnm|1a1=Musto|1y=2014|1pp=60–63|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=192}} However, it was plagued by internal divisions and strategic sterility. Its refusal to engage in the political struggles of the day isolated it from the broader working-class movement, which was increasingly organizing into national social democratic parties.{{sfn|Stekloff|1928|pp=302, 323}} Its focus on "[[Propaganda of the deed|propaganda by deed]]"—instigating futile local insurrections, such as the [[1874 Bologna insurrection plot|Bologna uprising of 1874]] and the [[Banda del Matese|Benevento putsch of 1877]]—proved disastrous.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1pp=311, 315|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1966|2p=218}} The movement also faced internal debates over the nature of a post-state society, with figures like the Belgian [[César De Paepe]] advocating for a federal "People's State" (''Volksstaat''), a position that satisfied neither the pure anarchists nor the Marxists.{{sfn|Cole|1954|pp=221–223}} By the late 1870s, the Anarchist International had effectively disintegrated, with its last congress held at Verviers in 1877.{{sfnm|1a1=Stekloff|1y=1928|1p=336|2a1=Cole|2y=1954|2p=225|3a1=Braunthal|3y=1966|3p=192}}

