==Organization==
The Comintern was designed to be a centralized "world party".{{sfnm|1a1=McDermott|1a2=Agnew|1y=1996|1p=23|2a1=Cole|2y=1958|2p=311}} Its supreme body was the World Congress, which was supposed to meet annually (later less frequently) to decide on program and policy.{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|pp=23, 225}} Between congresses, the Comintern was directed by its [[Executive Committee of the Communist International|Executive Committee]] (ECCI).{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|pp=14, 225}} The ECCI, in turn, elected a Presidium to handle day-to-day affairs, and a Secretariat.{{sfnm|1a1=McDermott|1a2=Agnew|1y=1996|1p=24|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1967|2p=263}} Other important bodies included the Organisational Bureau (Orgburo) and the International Control Commission (ICC), which was responsible for discipline and ideological purity.{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=24}} The [[International Liaison Department of the Communist International|International Liaison Department]] ({{lang|ru-latn|Otdel mezhdunarodnoi sviazi}}, OMS), established in 1921, managed the Comintern's clandestine activities, including funding, communications, and forging documents.{{sfnm|1a1=McDermott|1a2=Agnew|1y=1996|1p=22|2a1=McKnight|2y=2012|2pp=vii, 52, 60, 61–62, 119–120|3a1=Lazitch|3a2=Drachkovitch|3y=1973|3p=xxvii}} The ECCI and its subsidiary bodies were based in Moscow.{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=xxi}} The statutes stipulated that the Communist Party of the host country (the Soviet Union) would have a disproportionate influence, holding five seats on the ECCI, while other major parties held one.{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=23}} National communist parties were considered "sections" of the Comintern, bound by its decisions.{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=18}}

By the early 1930s, the parties' social composition had shifted significantly. Following the "[[Third Period]]" policy and the [[Great Depression]], many parties were transformed from organizations of employed industrial workers into parties of the unemployed. In Germany, the percentage of factory workers in the KPD dropped from over 62% in 1928 to around 20% in 1931.{{sfn|Borkenau|1962|p=364}} The parties were also characterized by an extremely high membership turnover, or "fluctuation". The entire membership of most parties was almost completely renewed every few years, with only a small nucleus of about 5% remaining constant, preventing the formation of stable traditions and cadres independent of Moscow.{{sfnm|1a1=Borkenau|1y=1962|1pp=366, 370–375|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1967|2p=300}} Between 1921 and 1931, the total membership of the Comintern's non-Soviet parties declined from 887,000 to 328,000.{{sfn|Claudín|1975|p=120}} For context, the rival [[Labour and Socialist International]] claimed an affiliated membership of over 6.2 million in 1928, with a total electoral vote of 25.6 million.{{sfnm|1a1=Cole|1y=1958|1p=690|2a1=Braunthal|2y=1967|2p=318}} The Comintern's finances, however, far exceeded those of its socialist rival; its income in 1927 was over twenty-six times greater, drawing on the resources of the Soviet state.{{sfn|Braunthal|1967|pp=319–320}}

