===Sponsored organizations===
[[File:Communist-International-1920.jpg|thumb|upright|1920 Russian issue of the ''[[Communist International (magazine)|Communist International]]'', the official magazine of the Comintern published in various European languages from 1919 to 1943]]

Several international organizations ([[communist front]]s) were sponsored by the Comintern:{{sfn|Lazitch|Drachkovitch|1973|pp=xviii–xxvii}}
* [[Young Communist International]] (KIM, 1919–1943), founded in Berlin under [[Willi Münzenberg]]
* [[Profintern|Red International of Labour Unions]] (Profintern, 1920–1937), designed to fight the [[International Federation of Trade Unions|"Amsterdam" International]]
* [[Communist Women's International]] (1920–1930)
* [[Workers International Relief]] (Mezhrabpom, 1921–1935), founded by Münzenberg in 1921 to coordinate [[Russian famine of 1921–1922|famine relief efforts in Soviet Russia]]
* [[Red Sport International]] (Sportintern, 1921–1937)
* [[International Red Aid]] (MOPR, 1922–1941), an organization to provide material and moral support to "captives of capitalism in prison"
* [[Krestintern|Red Peasant International]] (Krestintern, 1923–1939), founded as a rival to the [[International Agrarian Bureau|"Green" Peasant International]]
* International of the Proletarian Freethinkers (1925–1933)
* [[League Against Imperialism and Colonial Oppression]] (1927–1935)

===Bureaus and training schools===
In addition to its central apparatus in Moscow, the Comintern established several regional bureaus to coordinate its activities. Among the most important was the [[Berlin]]-based West European Secretariat (WES), founded in October 1919 under the leadership of [[Yakov Reich]] ("Thomas").{{sfnm|1a1=Studer|1y=2023|1p=98|2a1=Lazitch|2a2=Drachkovitch|2y=1973|2p=xxvii}} It served as a critical hub for communications, finance, and propaganda, channeling funds (including cash and diamonds) and directives from Moscow to the emerging communist parties in Europe.{{sfn|Studer|2023|pp=98–100}} In the early 1920s, Weimar Berlin, with its large working-class movement and relatively loose controls, became the Comintern's most important outpost and a center for its transnational network of agents.{{sfn|Studer|2023|p=126}} The WES was reorganized in 1927 as the West European Bureau (WEB), with a much clearer line of political control from Moscow.{{sfn|Studer|2023|p=97}} Other bureaus included the Scandinavian Bureau, a Southern Bureau in [[Kiev]], the Vienna Bureau, the Balkan Bureau, the [[Amsterdam Bureau of the Communist International|Amsterdam Bureau]],<ref>{{Cite news |last=Kókai |first=Károly |date=2017 |title=The Communist International and the Contribution of Georg Lukács in the 1920s |work=Social Scientist |jstor=26405282 }}</ref> the Central Asian Bureau in [[Tashkent]], and the [[Far Eastern Bureau of the Communist International|Far Eastern Bureau]], based for much of its existence in [[Shanghai]].{{sfn|Studer|2023|p=282}}

A crucial part of the Comintern's structure was its system of political schools, designed to train cadres from around the world in communist theory and practice. The immense importance of this schooling was a key feature of the organization, as it produced a worldwide cadre of loyal communists.{{sfn|Lazitch|Drachkovitch|1973|p=xiv}} The four principal schools were:{{sfn|Lazitch|Drachkovitch|1973|p=xiii}}
* [[Communist University of the Toilers of the East]] (KUTV, 1921–1937) provided political education for cadres from Soviet Eastern republics and later admitted candidates from colonial and independent countries in the Middle East and Eastern Mediterranean.{{sfn|Lazitch|Drachkovitch|1973|p=xxv}}
* [[Communist University of the National Minorities of the West]] (KUNMZ, 1921–1936) initially trained cadres from the western national minorities of Soviet Russia, but later expanded to include militants from the Balkans, Italy, Central Europe, and Scandinavia.{{sfn|Lazitch|Drachkovitch|1973|p=xxv}}
* [[Moscow Sun Yat-sen University]] (1925–1930) specialized in training Chinese revolutionaries.{{sfn|Studer|2023|pp=295–296, 303}}
* [[International Lenin School]] (1926–1938), the Comintern's most advanced political school, training leading cadres from various communist parties.{{sfn|Studer|2023|pp=296, 303}}

==Historiography==
The [[historiography]] of the Comintern is diverse and has evolved significantly, particularly with the opening of Soviet archives in the late 1980s.{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=xvii}} Early Western scholarship during the [[Cold War]] often depicted the Comintern as a monolithic tool of Soviet foreign policy,{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=xxii}} and found official Soviet historiography sanitized and ideologically controlled.{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|pp=xxii, xxiv}} Dissident communist critiques, such as those by former members like [[Franz Borkenau]] or [[Trotskyist]] writers, often focused on the Comintern's degeneration under Stalin compared to an idealized Leninist phase.{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=xxii}}{{sfn|Borkenau|1962|pp=10, 12, 16}} 

Other studies, including those by [[E. H. Carr]] and [[Fernando Claudin]], offer more nuanced interpretations.{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|pp=xxii-xxiv}} Carr analyzed the Comintern's relationship with Soviet foreign policy, allowing for a degree of autonomous action by Comintern leaders and national parties.{{sfnm|1a1=McDermott|1a2=Agnew|1y=1996|1p=xxiii|2a1=Carr|2y=1982|2pp=vii–viii, 3–6}} Claudin, in his Marxist analysis, argued that the Comintern was marked by a "crisis of theory" from its inception, as it failed to differentiate between Russian and Western European conditions leading to flawed strategies.{{sfnm|1a1=McDermott|1a2=Agnew|1y=1996|1p=xxiii|2a1=Claudín|2y=1975|2pp=44, 99}}

The opening of the Comintern archives in Moscow spurred new research, often confirming the extent of Soviet control, particularly under Stalin, but also revealing internal debates within the Comintern apparatus and complexities in the relationship between the center and national sections.{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|pp=xvii, xxiv}} There is ongoing debate about the degree of autonomy retained by national parties and the interplay between Moscow's directives and indigenous factors in shaping communist policies.{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|pp=xx-xxi}} More recent scholarship has also adopted a transnational and global history perspective, focusing on the Comintern as a network and on the lives and experiences of cadres operating across borders, treating the organization as a unique "lifeworld" with its own practices and culture.{{sfn|Studer|2023|pp=24, 30–31, 419–420}}

==Legacy==
The Comintern's legacy is another site of ongoing debate. In the 1920s it nurtured a range of theoretical responses to contemporary problems from figures like Trotsky, Bukharin, and [[Antonio Gramsci]].{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=216}} Opponents claim the Comintern transformed from an instrument for world revolution into an instrument of Soviet foreign policy, particularly under Stalin,{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|pp=213-214}} undergoing processes of [[Bolshevisation]] and centralisation that led to the demotion, expulsion, and purging of those who resisted Moscow's line.{{sfn|Borkenau|1962|pp=418, 423}} {{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=214}} The Comintern supported the Soviet Union through contentious acts like the [[Great Purge]] and the [[Nazi–Soviet Pact]],{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=214}} and its hostility towards [[syndicalism]] and other currents of revolutionary socialism drove out figures like [[Alfred Rosmer]], [[Pierre Monatte]], [[Ángel Pestaña]], and [[Martin Tranmæl]].{{sfn|Cole|1961|p=319}} [[Democratic socialism|Democratic socialist]] historian [[G. D. H. Cole]] argues that the Comintern's "[[social fascism]]" theory helped fascism come to power in Italy and Germany by deliberately setting out to split the world's socialist movements, seeing reformists and centrists as "social traitors" and dividing working class forces.{{sfn|Cole|1961|p=38}}

Trotskyists and other anti-Soviet Leninists claim the Comintern universalised a Bolshevik model specific to Russian conditions, the core reason for the Comintern's failures{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=219}} and a Stalinist "ossification" of Marxist thought that hindered the development of strategies more applicable to diverse national conditions.{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=214}} Others blame not just Stalin but Leninism itself, finding the Leninist party structure and its associated dogmas an enduring constraint on communist parties adapting to changing post-war realities.{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=218}} Socialist historian [[Julius Braunthal]] argued that Lenin's doctrine "wrecked the unity of the international workers movement... and, in Italy and Germany, paved the way for Fascism".{{sfn|Braunthal|1980|p=504}}{{sfn|Braunthal|1967|pp=389–390}}

The Comintern ultimately failed to achieve its original aims of worldwide socialist revolution and colonial liberation,{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=213}} overseeing a series of disappointments such as the [[German October]] of 1923 and the [[1926 United Kingdom general strike|British general strike of 1926]].{{sfn|Borkenau|1962|p=413}} Nonetheless, the Comintern represented a unique historical experiment in creating a global and transnational network supporting emancipatory movements, and gave a voice to oppressed groups such as the working class, women, and the peoples of the colonies.{{sfn|Studer|2023|p=426}} During the [[Popular Front]] era and [[World War II]], communists were among the most active anti-fascists, notably organizing the [[International Brigades]] in the [[Spanish Civil War]], though this support was also used to enforce Soviet orthodoxy and suppress other left-wing groups.{{sfnm|1a1=McDermott|1a2=Agnew|1y=1996|1p=217|2a1=Carr|2y=1986|2pp=42–44}} The threat of communism may also have spurred capitalist governments to undertake social reforms.{{sfn|McDermott|Agnew|1996|p=217}} After World War II, communism expanded significantly, with organized communists numbering fourteen million outside the Soviet Union by the end of 1945. This growth was partly due to the Soviet war effort and the groundwork laid by the Comintern in consolidating disciplined communist parties.{{sfnm|1a1=McDermott|1a2=Agnew|1y=1996|1p=217|2a1=Claudín|2y=1975|2p=307}}

