The '''European Left Alliance for the People and the Planet''' (ELA) is a [[European political party]] formed by several political parties already connected within the framework of [[Now the People]] (NTP), a left-wing [[European political alliances|European political alliance]] formed in 2018.<ref>{{Cite web |title=AGENCE EUROPE - A new European feminist and radical left party is being form... |url=https://agenceurope.eu/en/bulletin/article/13472/5 |access-date=2024-09-02 |website=agenceurope.eu}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Registered Parties|url=https://www.appf.europa.eu/appf/en/parties-and-foundations/registered-parties |access-date=2024-09-27 |website=Authority for European Political Parties and European Political Foundations |language=en}}</ref>

The ELA consists of nine national-level political parties, which tend to be newer, green-left leaning parties.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Neivelt |first=Jonas |date=2024-08-27 |title=Enhedslisten forlader European Left og går med i nyt europæisk parti |url=https://solidaritet.dk/enhedslisten-forlader-european-left-og-gaar-med-i-nyt-europaeisk-parti/ |access-date=2024-08-30 |website=Solidaritet |language=da-DK}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-08-30 |title=Will they name names? |url=https://www.politico.eu/newsletter/brussels-playbook/will-they-name-names/ |access-date=2024-08-30 |website=POLITICO |language=en-GB}}</ref>

== History ==
=== Foundation ===
In 2024, the Danish member Red-Green Alliance ([[Red–Green Alliance (Denmark)|Enhedlisten]]) announced its schism from the [[Party of the European Left]] and the formation of a socially liberal (green- and pro-Ukraine leaning) European Left Alliance. The split follows the split of socially liberal [[The Left (Germany)|Die Linke]] and socially conservative [[Bündnis Sahra Wagenknecht|BSW]] in Germany, and the creation of a separate platform for the 2024 European elections, Now the People.<ref name=":0"/>

The first officially confirmed member of the ELA was the Red-Green Alliance of Denmark.<ref name=":0" /> Other founder members were Portugal's [[Left Bloc (Portugal)|Left Bloc]], [[La France Insoumise]], the Swedish [[Left Party (Sweden)|Left Party]], Finland's [[Left Alliance (Finland)|Left Alliance]], Poland's [[Partia Razem]] and Spain's [[Podemos (Spanish political party)|Podemos]].<ref name="Euronews_1192024"/>

In the European Parliament, [[Member of the European Parliament|MEPs]] of both PEL ([[Party of the European Left]]) and the ELA belong to the parliamentary group of [[The Left in the European Parliament – GUE/NGL]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |date=2024-09-02 |title=Una desolante divisione della sinistra in Europa |url=https://ilmanifesto.it/una-desolante-divisione-della-sinistra-in-europa |access-date=2024-09-03 |website=il manifesto |language=it}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=redazione |date=2024-09-03 |title=In Europa una desolante divisione della sinistra • Diritti Globali |url=https://www.dirittiglobali.it/2024/09/in-europa-una-desolante-divisione-della-sinistra/ |access-date=2024-09-08 |website=Diritti Globali |language=it-IT}}</ref>

Factions of Germany's [[Die Linke]] submitted a motion to express its loyalty towards PEL claiming that the "fragmentation of the European left at a time of the rise of the extreme right is a mistake."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://die-zukunft.eu/die-zersplitterung-der-europaeischen-linken-ist-ein-fehler/|title="Die Zersplitterung der europäischen Linken ist ein Fehler"|language=de|date=2024-09-06|access-date=2024-09-12|author=Uwe Sattler|work=die-zukunft.eu}}</ref>

On 27 September 2024, the [[Authority for European Political Parties and European Political Foundations]] formally registered the ELA as a European political party.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.appf.europa.eu/appf/en/european-left-alliance-for-the-people-an/products-details/20240927CDT13661 |title=European Left Alliance for the People and the Planet |language=en |date=2024-09-27 |access-date=2024-09-30 |website=Authority for European Political Parties and European Political Foundations}}</ref>

===First Congress===

The European Left Alliance's first Congress took place in [[Porto]] on 13–14 June 2025.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://leftalliance.eu/agenda/first-congress/|title=First Congress of the European Left Alliance: For a stronger left against the far-right |language=en |access-date=21 June 2025}}</ref> During the Congress, two political parties joined the ELA as associate members: the Basque [[EH Bildu|Euskal Herria Bildu]] (EH Bildu) and the Norwegian [[Socialist Left Party (Norway)|Socialist Left Party]]. In addition to the seven founding members of ELA and to these two associate members, several left-wing political parties attended the Congress, including [[Die Linke]] from Germany, [[Italian Left|Sinistra Italiana]] from Italy, [[The Left (Luxembourg)|Dei Lenk]] from Luxembourg, [[We Can! (Croatia)|Mozemo]] from Croatia, [[Democracy and Solidarity Party|Demos]] from Romania, [[Budoucnost]] from Czechia and [[Peoples' Equality and Democracy Party|DEM Party]] from Turkey. The Congress decided to initiate the creation of a [[European political foundation]], as well as a [[European Citizens' Initiative]] to "address the situation in Gaza".<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.euractiv.com/section/politics/news/european-left-alliance-launches-in-porto-in-a-bid-to-counter-far-right-surge-across-europe/ |title=European Left Alliance launches in Porto in a bid to counter the far right surge across Europe |date=14 June 2025}}</ref>

===Political foundation===

In August 2025, the European Left Alliance established its affiliated [[European political foundation]], For the People,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.appf.europa.eu/cmsdata/298498/Pour%20le%20Peuple%20-%20Application%20Letter.pdf|title=Letter of application for registration as a European Political Foundation |date=28 August 2025 |website=Authority for European Political Parties and European Political Foundations}}</ref> which was registered by the [[Authority for European Political Parties and European Political Foundations]] on 30 September 2025.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.appf.europa.eu/appf/en/pour-le-peuple-ftp-/products-details/20250930CDT14685|title=Pour le Peuple ('FTP') |website=Authority for European Political Parties and European Political Foundations |access-date=1 October 2025}}</ref> The co-directors of the foundation are the Finnish [[Member of the European Parliament|MEP]] [[Li Andersson]] and the French [[Deputy (France)|MP]] [[Clémence Guetté]], and its coordinator is former Portuguese MEP [[Marisa Matias]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Welcome - For the People Foundation |url=https://forthepeoplefoundation.eu/#board |access-date=8 April 2026 |date=5 March 2026}}</ref> The foundation's activities officially started in April 2026 with a launch event gathering political leaders and academics.<ref>{{cite web |title=For the People launch event: sovereignty, social justice and action|url=https://forthepeoplefoundation.eu/launch-event/ |access-date=14 April 2026}}</ref>

===Expansion===

In December 2025, [[Sinistra Italiana]] joined the European Left Alliance as a full member. This decision follows on from Sinistra Italiana's membership of Now the People (NTP) and its participation in ELA's first Congress in June 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sinistra italiana aderisce all’alleanza europea Ela |url=https://ilmanifesto.it/sinistra-italiana-aderisce-allalleanza-europea-ela |date=10 December 2025 |website=Il Manifesto |language=it}}</ref> In January 2026, the Dutch left-wing [[Socialist Party (Netherlands)|Socialist Party]] joined the European Left Alliance as a full member.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.instagram.com/p/DUBTqE2irKP/|title=Instagram|website=www.instagram.com}}</ref>

On 13 January 2026, the European Left Alliance officially launched a [[European Citizens' Initiative]] (ECI) to suspend the [[Israel–European Union relations#EU–Israel Association Agreement (2000)|EU-Israel Association Agreement]] in order to sanction Israel’s massive violations of human rights in [[Palestine]], especially during the [[Gaza war]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://europeannewsroom.com/podemos-and-other-left-wing-parties-collect-signatures-for-the-eu-to-break-off-relations-with-israel/ |title=Podemos and other left-wing parties collect signatures for the EU to break off relations with Israel |date=13 January 2026}}</ref> The ECI was registered in November 2025 and the collection of signatures started on 13 January 2026.<ref name=":3">{{cite web |url=https://citizens-initiative.europa.eu/initiatives/details/2025/000005_en |title=Demand the full suspension of the EU-Israel Association Agreement in view of Israel’s violations of human rights |date=14 June 2025}}</ref>

==Ideology==
The party postulates unity of "European green, feminist, and anti-racist left" and intends to create "new forms of solidarity work and prepare concrete actions of mobilization against capitalism and against war".<ref>{{cite web |language=es |url=https://vientosur.info/resistir-recuperarse-y-ampliar-alianzas-para-luchar-con-determinacion/ |website=[[:es:Viento Sur|Viento Sur]] |title=Resistir, recuperarse y ampliar alianzas para luchar con determinación |date=31 May 2025}}</ref> It has been described to bring together parties from "a spectrum of democratic socialism, eco-socialism, social democracy and feminism".<ref name="lewica"/> The alliance excludes communist parties from its ranks, and aims to eliminate the "old left linked to communist parties, which they consider a hindrance to their electoral expansion".<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.abc.es/espana/pablo-iglesias-impulsa-nueva-izquierda-europea-sumar-20241022040742-nt.html |language=es |website=[[ABC (newspaper)|ABC]] |title=Pablo Iglesias impulsa una nueva izquierda europea sin Sumar |date=22 October 2024 |first=Enrique |last=Serbeto |location=Brussels}}</ref>

Many of its member parties are described as [[Left-wing populism|left-wing populist]].<ref name="leftpopulist"/>

The party supports Palestinians' rights to self-determination, and supports sanctions of Israel over its [[Gaza genocide|genocide in Gaza]].<ref name=":3" />

== Members ==
20 of the 46 MEPs who compose the Left group in the European Parliament are members of political parties which participate in ELA as full members, in addition to one MEP from a political party that is an associate members of ELA (EH Bildu).<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=2024-09-03 |title=Brat summer ends in sausage fest |url=https://www.politico.eu/newsletter/brussels-playbook/brat-summer-ends-in-sausage-fest/ |access-date=2024-09-03 |website=POLITICO |language=en-GB}}</ref>

