The '''International Committee of the Fourth International''' ('''ICFI''') is a [[political international]] initially founded as a public faction of the [[Fourth International]] in 1953. The faction withdrew from the Fourth International (referred to by the ICFI as the [[International Secretariat of the Fourth International|International Secretariat]]) to hold their own separate congresses.

The ICFI split in 1961 with the French [[Internationalist Communist Organisation|Internationalist Communist Party]] (PCI) and British [[Socialist Labour League]] (SLL) continuing the ICFI after some sections of the organisation [[Fourth International (post-reunification)|reunified]] in 1963. Further splits occurred in 1971 between the SLL and PCI, and then again in the late-1980s when the SLL (by then called the [[Workers Revolutionary Party (UK)|Workers Revolutionary Party]], WRP) collapsed. 

Currently, two [[Trotskyist]] [[List of Trotskyist internationals|internationals]] claim to be organisers of the ICFI: the [[orthodox Trotskyist]] [[Socialist Equality Party]] (SEP), a splinter group of the WRP founded in 1986, and [[Sheila Torrance]]'s Workers Revolutionary Party formed after the collapse of the original WRP.{{cn|date=September 2026}}

The SEP-ICFI has a number of national sections, all organising under the name Socialist Equality Party. Notable sections with their own articles are those in the [[Socialist Equality Party (United States)|United States]], [[Socialist Equality Party (Australia)|Australia]], and [[Socialist Equality Party (Germany)|Germany]].

==History==
===1938-61: The Fourth International and the formation of the ICFI===
The [[Fourth International]] (FI) was founded in 1938 by [[Leon Trotsky]] and his followers. The FI had previously been organised as the [[International Left Opposition]] since 1923 within the [[Communist International]]. Its American section was the [[Socialist Workers Party (United States)|Socialist Workers Party]] (SWP), led by [[James P. Cannon]]. Its British section was the [[Revolutionary Socialist League (UK, 1938)|Revolutionary Socialist League]], which merged with the smaller [[Workers' International League (1937)|Workers' International League]] (WIL) in 1944 to form the [[Revolutionary Communist Party (UK, 1944)|Revolutionary Communist Party]] (RCP). One of the leaders of the WIL was [[Gerry Healy]], who would later split from the RCP to form "the Club", which became the Socialist Labour League, later (from 1973) called [[Workers Revolutionary Party (UK)|Workers Revolutionary Party]] (WRP). Cannon and Healy were close politically.{{citation needed|date=August 2026}}

On 3-4 October 1953, representatives from The Club, the Internationalist Communist Party, the Swiss section of the FI and a member of the SWP met to form a faction opposing [[Pabloism]] and [[liquidationism]].{{sfn|Alexander|1991|p=535}} At a meeting on 7-8 November 1953, the US SWP came into conflict with the FI's leadership for expelling a faction within their party, and was criticised in a letter written on 16 November for undermining the unity and principles of the FI.{{sfn|Alexander|1991|pp=535-536}}

In the same meeting, the SWP adopted ''A Letter to Trotskyists Throughout the World''.{{sfn|Alexander|1991|p=536}} which was then published in [[The Militant]] on 16 November 1953.<ref name="SWP">{{Cite magazine|author=Socialist Workers Party|author-link=Socialist Workers Party (US)|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150314212421/http://www.marxists.org/history/etol/document/swp-us/doc01.htm|archive-date=14 March 2015|url=http://www.marxists.org/history/etol/document/swp-us/doc01.htm|title=A Letter to Trotskyists Throughout the World|magazine=[[The Militant]]|date=16 November 1953 |via=[[Marxist Internet Archive]]}}</ref> The SWP's letter, which criticised [[Michel Pablo]]'s leadership and urged sections to demand the removal of him and his allies, was backed by the "Resolution Forming the International Committee" published on 23 November 1953. The resolution had been signed by the sections that had met in October, along with a member from a New Zealand section who was allegedly actually [[Farrell Dobbs]] of the US SWP.{{sfn|Alexander|1991|p=536}} The resolution officially organised the faction into the International Committee of the Fourth International (ICFI), claiming to represent the majority of [[Trotskyists]] in the Fourth International.{{sfn|Alexander|1991|p=537}}

In response, the International Executive Committee (IEC) of the Fourth International suspended members in the IEC who signed either declaration or were in leadership roles of supporting organisations. This meeting, held on 26-28 December, also recognised the expelled minorities of the US and UK sections as the legitimate sections and replaced [[Gerry Healy]] from his positions in favour of [[John Lawrence (political activist)|John Lawrence]].{{sfn|Alexander|1991|p=537}}

Over the following decade, the ICFI referred to the rest of the International as the "International Secretariat of the Fourth International", emphasising its view that the Secretariat did not speak for the International as a whole.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.marxists.org/history/etol/document/fi/1950-1953/ic-issplit/01.htm |title=Resolution of the International Committee instructing publication of the documents, August 24, 1973 |magazine=[[News Line|Workers Press]] |date=29 August 1973 |via=[[Marxist Internet Archive]]}}</ref> While presenting it self as an international, the group did not hold full congresses and was described by [[Pierre Frank]] as acting more like a political faction.{{sfn|Alexander|1991|p=537}}

Following the [[Hungarian Revolution of 1956]], the ICFI published a manifesto in November of the same year supporting the uprising against [[Stalinism]].{{sfn|Alexander|1991|p=539}}

A world conference was held in 1958 in [[Leeds]] and was led by the SWP's delegates. [[Latin America|Latin American]] delegates complained of the SWP's attitudes towards the ICFI, and their stance on reunification with the International Secretariat.{{sfn|Alexander|1991|p=538}}

By 1961 the ICFI had split politically: Lambert’s [[Internationalist Communist Organisation|Internationalist Communist Party]] (PCI) in France and Healy’s [[Socialist Labour League]] (SLL) in Britain argued that a [[workers' state]] had not been created in [[Cuba]], putting them at odds with the American SWP and the other organisations in the ICFI.<ref>The International Trotskyist Movement and the Postwar Revolutions: An Analysis of its Theoretical and Programmatic (Re)Interpretations (1944–1963), Marcio Lauria Monteiro</ref>{{full citation needed|date=August 2026}} By 1963, the split was also organizational. Each side held a congress at which it claimed to be the majority of the ICFI.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.marxists.org/history/etol/document/wsl/PoisonedWell79.htm |title=The Poisoned Well |author=[[Workers' Socialist League]] |date=July 1978 |via=[[Marxist Internet Archive]]}}</ref> On the one hand, the remaining Austrian, Chinese and New Zealand IC groups, which had not already unified, met at a congress with the SWP and voted to take part in the reunification congress. On the other hand, the PCI and SLL called an "International Conference of Trotskyists" to continue the work of the ICFI under their own leadership.<ref>[https://fileserver.iire.org/working_papers/WPs%2017%20feb/PDFfrench070.PDF Combats et débats de la IVe Internationale -'I De la réunification de 1963 à nos jours], François Moreau, juillet 1990</ref>

===1962-1985: The reunified International and the split on ICFI===
In 1962 the International Committee and International Secretariat (the term that the ICFI used to refer to the Fourth International Secretariat led by Michel Pablo) formed a Parity Commission to organise a common World Congress.{{citation needed|date=August 2026}}

At the June 1963 reunification congress held in Italy, the two sections reunified, electing the [[United Secretariat of the Fourth International]] (USFI) to replace the former groups.<ref name="Farrell">{{cite journal|author-first1=Farrell |author-last1=Dobbs |author-link1=Farrell Dobbs |author-first2=Joseph |author-last2=Hansen |author-link2=Joseph Hansen (socialist) |url=https://www.marxists.org/history/etol/document/swp-us/misc-1/reunif.htm |title=Reunification of the Fourth International |journal=[[International Socialist Review (1956)|International Socialist Review]] |date=Fall 1963 |via=[[Marxist Internet Archive]]}}</ref> The British and French parties in the ICFI did not join this reunified congress, as they believed that Michel Pablo's ideas should be completely rejected before reunification could occur.{{sfn|Kelly|2018|p=44}}

The remainder of the ICFI held a conference opposing the USFI in April 1966, hosted in [[London]].{{sfn|Alexander|1991|p=539}}

By 1971, the ICFI began collapsing due to disagreements between [[Gerry Healy]] and [[Pierre Lambert]]. The group met seven times between the early 1970s and 1985, and affiliates numbered maximum 10–12.{{sfn|Kelly|2018|p=49}} In 1971/1972, the IC split in two. The [[Socialist Labour League]] under Gerry Healy kept the name while the [[Internationalist Communist Organisation]] under [[Pierre Lambert]] left to create the [[Organising Committee for the Reconstruction of the Fourth International]] (CORQI).{{sfn|Alexander|1991|p=21}}

===1985: after Healy===
{{See also|Workers Revolutionary Party (UK)#Expulsion of Gerry Healy}}
In 1985, Healy was expelled from the WRP.<ref name="Times241085">{{cite news |url=https://www.thetimes.com/tto/archive/article/1985-10-24/2/12.html |title=Workers' Party Founder is Thrown Out |work=[[The Times]] |location=London |date=24 October 1985 |access-date=10 May 2016}} {{subscription required}}</ref> This had come as a result of several allegations of sexual assault made against him.<ref name="Spencer">{{cite news |title=Bedtime stories |last=Spencer |first=John |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=10 September 1994}}</ref>

The WRP collapsed into ten smaller organisations{{sfn|Kelly|2018|p=61}} One faction, under the leadership of [[Sheila Torrance]], retained the name WRP and daily newspaper ''[[The News Line]]''. A further split from this, in 1987, was the Healy-loyal [[Marxist Party]], including [[Vanessa Redgrave|Vanessa]] and [[Corin Redgrave]].<ref>{{Citation |last=Gregson |first=John |chapter=The Revolutionary Marxists: The Socialist Labour League and International Socialism |date=2019 |chapter-url=http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-030-03371-2_4 |title=Marxism, Ethics and Politics |series=Marx, Engels, and Marxisms |pages=89–134 |place=Cham |publisher=Springer International Publishing |doi=10.1007/978-3-030-03371-2_4 |isbn=978-3-030-03370-5 |s2cid=158748605 |access-date=2022-08-30 |chapter-url-access=subscription}}</ref> Both groupuscules maintained small international organisations claiming the name ICFI, the former still extant, the latter moribund by the late 1990s.{{citation needed|date=August 2026}}

In 1986 Dave Hyland broke away from the WRP to form the International Communist Party, which he led until his death in 2013.{{sfn|Kelly|2018|p=61}} This group maintained itself as [[Orthodox Trotskyist]],{{sfn|Kelly|2018|p=72}} and was backed by the US Workers League National Secretary, [[David North (socialist)|David North]]. This group created their own ICFI, with groups in Australia, Germany, Sri Lanka, the UK, and the USA. In 2016, the group also expanded to France and New Zealand.{{sfn|Kelly|2018|pp=61–62}}

This group supported the perspective advanced by ICFI and won the support of a majority of sections of the ICFI to expel the WRP from the organization, deciding that any supporters of theirs among the WRP would have to undergo a reregistration of membership based on recognizing the ICFI's political authority to be readmitted.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.wsws.org/en/special/library/how-the-wrp-betrayed-trotskyism/00.html|title=How the Workers Revolutionary Party Betrayed Trotskyism|website=World Socialist Web Site}}</ref> They argued that “the WRP tended to view the international organization as little more than an adjunct to its own British-based organization”.<ref>{{Cite web |title=A Further Comment on the Cause and Significance of the Split in the ICFI |url=https://www.wsws.org/en/special/library/foundations-us/54.html |access-date=2026-04-15 |website=World Socialist Web Site}}</ref>

In 1995, the International Communist Party renamed itself to the Socialist Equality Party. In 1998, the ICFI launched the [[World Socialist Web Site]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=About the WSWS |url=https://www.wsws.org/en/special/pages/icfi/wsws.html |access-date=2026-09-18 |website=World Socialist Web Site}}</ref> ICFI runs the publishing house [[Mehring Books]], formerly named Labor Publications.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20161025052132/http://mehring.com/about "About Mehring Books,"], October 2016.</ref> In 2006, the Socialist Equality Party relaunched its student movement (the Students for Social Equality) as the International Students for Social Equality (ISSE). In 2012, the SEP renamed the ISSE as the International Youth and Students for Social Equality (IYSSE).<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2012/09/res4-s03.html |title=Resolutions of the SEP Congress: Build the International Youth and Students for Social Equality |date=September 3, 2012 |website=[[World Socialist Web Site]]}}</ref>
